                        "form": "genitiefjes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a2dc4013-3b52-3479-85b0-166ddfaf37ff",
                        "entry_id": "2e4d595f-3c01-3e96-8e29-73f7143fe1b2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "een van de acht naamvallen van de Indo-Europese talen die oorspronkelijk een oorzakelijk voorwerp aanduidde, later vooral een bezitsrelatie"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6a03a1c1-d0fd-481b-8255-b617fdfbeecc",
                                "sense_id": "a2dc4013-3b52-3479-85b0-166ddfaf37ff",
                                "text": "De genitief is de tweede naamval in de meeste Europese talen en wordt gebruikt om aan te geven van wie of wat iets is."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b9cc3eb1-11cc-4a5e-9d82-10391f7311d0",
                        "source_entry_id": "2e4d595f-3c01-3e96-8e29-73f7143fe1b2",
                        "source_sense_id": "a2dc4013-3b52-3479-85b0-166ddfaf37ff",
                        "source_sense_description": "naamval als aanduiding van bezit, oorsprong of hoeveelheid",
                        "target_lang_word": "Genitiv",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Genitiv",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b6be7ff3-fd2c-35eb-bbc2-4746e9072e68",
                                        "entry_id": "29b3f3b9-ca19-3299-b45a-8c765ff99ab6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Wes-Fall, (im Deutschen und Lateinischen) 2. Fall (Kasus)"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "662e04aa-b13c-4293-910f-b7972447bf08",
                                                "sense_id": "b6be7ff3-fd2c-35eb-bbc2-4746e9072e68",
                                                "text": "Der besitzanzeigende Genitiv (Genitivus possessivus) drückt aus, wessen Eigentum eine Sache ist."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f85bfb7b-54d1-436b-a663-cbf9dae12429",
                                                "sense_id": "b6be7ff3-fd2c-35eb-bbc2-4746e9072e68",
                                                "text": "„In letzter Zeit konnte man viele Klagen über den Verlust des Genitivs vernehmen […].“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "212554bd-0ca1-4293-bd24-57439189ad86",
                                                "sense_id": "b6be7ff3-fd2c-35eb-bbc2-4746e9072e68",
                                                "text": "„Der Genitiv kann eine Reihe von unterschiedlichen sprachlichen Leistungen erfüllen…“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "675a9b46-6393-43c7-ade2-5126f3c117e0",
                                                "sense_id": "b6be7ff3-fd2c-35eb-bbc2-4746e9072e68",
                                                "text": "„Unsere Eltern belegten einen Sprachkurs nach dem anderen, sie lernten unaufhörlich Vokabeln, sie waren irritiert, wenn sie merkten, dass die deutschen Nachbarn Dativ statt Genitiv benutzten, und schauten daheim in ihren Lehrbüchern nach.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2ed64743-b427-45b0-8c74-5f452c6ec077",
                                                "sense_id": "b6be7ff3-fd2c-35eb-bbc2-4746e9072e68",
                                                "text": "„Der Genitiv ist auf vielen Feldern auf dem Rückzug und wird durch den Dativ ersetzt.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8bf59f4b-2c5c-4e7b-9620-9266ddab6f4f",
                        "source_entry_id": "2e4d595f-3c01-3e96-8e29-73f7143fe1b2",
                        "source_sense_id": "a2dc4013-3b52-3479-85b0-166ddfaf37ff",
                        "source_sense_description": "naamval als aanduiding van bezit, oorsprong of hoeveelheid",
                        "target_lang_word": "genitive case",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "genitive case",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "687234f7-c0ec-337a-a22d-015b545ba353",
                                        "entry_id": "dd6acea5-b18d-3810-bff7-b50c44136cce",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The grammatical case, chiefly applicable to nouns, used to express a relationship of origin or possession. It corresponds roughly to the English preposition of and the suffix -'s."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cd1bc627-f4b0-40fd-b53d-0dd95021e4f5",
                                                "sense_id": "687234f7-c0ec-337a-a22d-015b545ba353",
                                                "text": "In very early times a man seems to have been known by a single name together with his father's name in the genitive case, e.g. Marcus Marci; […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d30511f0-e9db-48fd-9ab1-5f2caede9fd5",
                        "source_entry_id": "2e4d595f-3c01-3e96-8e29-73f7143fe1b2",
                        "source_sense_id": "a2dc4013-3b52-3479-85b0-166ddfaf37ff",
                        "source_sense_description": "naamval als aanduiding van bezit, oorsprong of hoeveelheid",
                        "target_lang_word": "genitive",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "genitive",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bcbc6e9a-c7e9-3e29-aab1-2f980c6b6c83",
                                        "entry_id": "30852f10-d5ed-3095-b4f0-53a70a67db99",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or pertaining to the generation of offspring; generative, procreative, reproductive."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic",
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                        "entry_id": "30852f10-d5ed-3095-b4f0-53a70a67db99",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of a grammatical case: in an inflected language (such as Greek or Latin), expressing that a thing denoted by a word is related to a thing denoted by another word as its origin or possessor; and in an uninflected language (such as English), expressing origin or possession; possessive."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79724a62-532b-4da8-8767-c5a163ed8314",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "dependent genitive"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3ca0fb84-7c54-4011-bb83-3e3dbfac7210",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "independent genitive"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fb5d77de-f4ee-4146-a474-872efc3381de",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "The student who had taken a German exam realised his error afterwards. He had used the dative case instead of the genitive case to show possession."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ca2a5932-94a8-4a70-84b1-f70e33c97de2",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "[T]he poticaries and barbarus wryters call it [the iris] Irios in the genetiue caſe."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a1fe4601-536a-4ca9-84c0-0a62cee07658",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "Nouns Subſtantive have five Declenſions or forms of ending thir Caſes, chiefly diſtinguiſht by the different ending of thir Genitive Singular. […] The firſt [declension] is vvhen the Genitive and Dative ſingular end in æ, &c., […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6efe6d39-717b-4123-bbb6-3f3d26f6392f",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "Two kinds of genitive: All nouns have a genitive form, but pronouns have two of them. The dependent genitive form is used before a head noun, and the independent genitive form (usually distinct) is used on its own but with a genitive-related meaning. […] my painting, where my is the dependent genitive of I, but That painting is mine, where mine is the independent genitive […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1c365cfb-9989-4347-a077-7109774fed26",
                                                "sense_id": "5b9be537-322c-3687-ac69-69dbe2547e90",
                                                "text": "Establishments that feature their owners' names, with signs like \"Rosi's Bar\" or \"Kati's Kiosk\" are a common sight around German towns and cities, but strictly speaking they are wrong: unlike English, German does not traditionally use apostrophes to indicate the genitive case or possession."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2cb9ab8b-7d12-3fdc-b3b3-df095da5aee2",
                                        "entry_id": "30852f10-d5ed-3095-b4f0-53a70a67db99",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of, pertaining to, or used in the genitive case."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "genitive",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9525648b-9997-30a4-bcfa-f2c499f9dcad",
                                        "entry_id": "bb37f161-56a7-34ea-90cc-973aebb0f27e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Ellipsis of genitive case (“a grammatical case used to express a relationship of origin or possession”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "abbreviation",
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "countable",
                                            "ellipsis",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1a78b015-9826-4344-a141-a88759991001",
                                                "sense_id": "9525648b-9997-30a4-bcfa-f2c499f9dcad",
                                                "text": "Nounes, Pronounes, and Participles are declin'd vvith ſix Endings, vvhich are called Caſes, both in the Singular and Plural Number. The Nominative, Genitive, Dative, Accuſative, Vocative, and Ablative. […] The Genitive is Engliſht vvith this Sign of, as Libri of a Book."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fa708f86-c154-303f-8306-f061b77b5a20",
                                        "entry_id": "bb37f161-56a7-34ea-90cc-973aebb0f27e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A word inflected in the genitive case, and which thus indicates origin or possession."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bee8a024-ed6e-4ce8-ada9-3922e0a4a63a",
                                                "sense_id": "fa708f86-c154-303f-8306-f061b77b5a20",
                                                "text": "This one vvord familia joyn'd vvith pater, mater, filius, or filia, endeth the Genitive in as, as pater familias, but ſomtimes familiæ."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "accaf518-abf2-4bcc-86f5-fd2a856fdbab",
                                                "sense_id": "fa708f86-c154-303f-8306-f061b77b5a20",
                                                "text": "This older kind of genetive [i.e., the direct genetive] is apparently expressed only by the position of the two substantives, in which the governing word stands before the governed: pr:Z1-i-mn:n-A40 pr i̓mn \"House of Amon.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b7a0cad5-62a1-4cda-bc39-33843e64960c",
                        "source_entry_id": "2e4d595f-3c01-3e96-8e29-73f7143fe1b2",
                        "source_sense_id": "a2dc4013-3b52-3479-85b0-166ddfaf37ff",
                        "source_sense_description": "naamval als aanduiding van bezit, oorsprong of hoeveelheid",
                        "target_lang_word": "génitif",
